{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789389436,"nonce":"uk8gr4K9V4ySEOsJKJ8FXmIVys0=","title":"An abort in a fiber run with call() does not reach the caller's try","message":"Reference Wren: an error in a fiber run with `call()` aborts the calling fiber too, and propagates up the chain until a `try()` catches it. wren_lift stops it early in both fiber backings, differently:\n\n```wren\nvar f = Fiber.new { Fiber.abort(\"boom\") }\nvar outer = Fiber.new {\n  var v = f.call()\n  System.print(\"after call: %(v)\")\n}\nvar r = outer.try()\nSystem.print(\"outer.try -\u003e %(r)\")\n```\n\nExpected (wren 0.4): `outer.try -\u003e boom`.\n\n- Frames-based fibers (default): `Error: boom` at the top level. The abort escapes `outer.try()` instead of being caught there.\n- krio fibers (`WLIFT_KRIO_FIBER=1`): `after call: null`, then `outer.try -\u003e after call: null`. The abort is swallowed: `f.call()` answers null and `outer` runs on.\n\nSame in both `wlift` modes and under caribou's runner (which turns krio fibers on). Found 2026-09-14 while checking fiber stacks on the core heap; `try()` directly around the aborting body does route correctly in both backings, which is what caribou's tests cover.","files":null}]}